    <!-- V. Chapter 4: Doctrinal Core: Shentong vs. Rangtong -->
    <section id="shentong-core" class="space-y-8 scroll-mt-24">
      <ChapterHeader
        chapter="Chapter 04"
        title="Doctrinal Core: Shentong (Other-Emptiness) vs. Rangtong"
        lede="At the heart of the Jonang tradition’s distinct identity is its philosophical formulation of emptiness (Śūnyatā), articulated through the dual framework of the Two Truths:"
      />

      <!-- Foundational Terminology Primer -->
      <div class="surface-card p-6 sm:p-8 md:p-10 space-y-6">
        <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-display text-ink font-normal m-0">
          Key Terms: What Do "Self-Nature" and "Buddha-Nature" Actually Mean?
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed font-serif m-0">
          Before examining the Rangtong–Shentong debate, two foundational concepts must be clearly understood. The entire disagreement turns on what these terms refer to and whether one of them points to something real:
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 pt-2">
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-surface-subtle border border-border space-y-3">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-accent uppercase tracking-wider">Term 1</span>
            </div>
            <h4 class="font-display text-base font-bold text-ink m-0">Self-Nature (Svabhāva / རང་བཞིན།)</h4>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0">
              <em>Svabhāva</em> literally means "own-being" or "self-nature." It refers to the hypothetical quality that would make something exist <strong>independently, from its own side, without depending on causes, conditions, parts, or a perceiving mind</strong>. A table, for instance, seems solidly real — but it depends on legs, a surface, wood, a carpenter, and a concept in the observer's mind. It has no autonomous essence holding it in existence. All everyday objects, thoughts, emotions, and even the sense of a personal self are like this: they appear, but nothing inside them exists on its own power.
            </p>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0">
              <strong>All Buddhist schools agree</strong> that ordinary phenomena lack <em>svabhāva</em>. This universal consensus — that tables, persons, feelings, and atoms are not self-sustaining — is the baseline meaning of "emptiness" (<em>Śūnyatā</em>).
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-surface-subtle border border-accent/30 space-y-3 bg-gradient-to-br from-surface-subtle to-gold/5">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-accent uppercase tracking-wider">Term 2</span>
            </div>
            <h4 class="font-display text-base font-bold text-ink m-0">Buddha-Nature (Tathāgatagarbha / བདེ་གཤེགས་སྙིང་པོ།)</h4>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0">
              <em>Tathāgatagarbha</em> literally means "the womb (or seed-essence) of the Thus-Gone One." It refers to <strong>whatever it is in every sentient being that makes the attainment of Buddhahood — the complete and irreversible awakening to the nature of reality, characterized by omniscient wisdom, unconditional compassion, and freedom from all suffering — possible</strong>. All Mahāyāna schools accept that something like this exists — otherwise the path would be pointless. But the schools disagree sharply about what it <em>is</em>.
            </p>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0">
              The critical question is: <strong>is Buddha-nature an unhewn block of marble</strong> — raw material with workability but no finished features, requiring years of a sculptor's labor (the path of merit and wisdom) to produce a statue that did not previously exist? <strong>Or is it a golden statue already complete beneath a thick layer of dried mud</strong> — fully formed and always present, needing only the removal of what covers it? The first image captures the Rangtong reading; the second, the Shentong reading. The disagreement is not about whether Buddha-nature is "real," but about whether the qualities of Buddhahood are <em>newly produced</em> by the path or <em>revealed</em> when adventitious obscurations are cleared away.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Two Truths Architecture Cards — Expanded -->
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        
        <div class="surface-card p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-5 border-l-4 border-l-accent">
          <div class="flex justify-between items-center">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-bold text-ink leading-snug m-0">Rangtong: "Empty of Self"</h3>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono text-muted uppercase">Conventional Truth</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed font-serif m-0">
            The Rangtong ("Self-Emptiness") position, championed most rigorously by the Gelug school following Nāgārjuna and Candrakīrti, holds that <strong>all phenomena without exception — including ultimate truth itself — are empty of self-nature (<em>svabhāva</em>)</strong>. Emptiness is defined as a <em>non-affirming negative</em> (<em>prasajya-pratiṣedha</em>): the sheer absence of inherent existence, full stop. It does not point to any hidden positive ground behind the negation.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed font-serif m-0">
            On this view, when the Buddha spoke of "Buddha-nature" in the Third Turning sūtras, he was using <strong>provisional, pedagogical language</strong> designed to encourage sentient beings — not describing an actually existent luminous entity. Buddha-nature is understood as the <em>mere emptiness of mind</em> (the fact that the mind, too, lacks inherent existence), combined with the <em>workability</em> to be transformed. Buddha-nature is the unhewn marble block: real material with genuine potential, but no finished features inside it yet. Whether a statue emerges depends entirely on the sculptor's sustained effort — the accumulation of merit and wisdom over the path. The qualities of a Buddha are <em>newly produced</em>, not released.
          </p>
          <div class="pt-3 border-t border-border text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed space-y-1">
            <span class="text-accent font-bold block text-caption-xs uppercase mb-1">What Rangtong denies:</span>
            <p class="m-0">That ultimate reality possesses any positive, inherent qualities of its own. Everything — including the deepest truth — is characterized by the bare absence of self-nature. There is nothing "behind" emptiness.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="surface-card p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-5 border-l-4 border-l-gold bg-gradient-to-br from-surface to-gold/5">
          <div class="flex justify-between items-center">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-bold text-ink leading-snug m-0">Shentong: "Empty of Other"</h3>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono text-accent uppercase">Ultimate Truth</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed font-serif m-0">
            The Shentong ("Other-Emptiness") position, systematized by the Jonang school following Dölpopa, <strong>fully agrees</strong> with Rangtong that all conventional phenomena are empty of self-nature. Tables, persons, thoughts, universes — all of these lack <em>svabhāva</em>. On this point there is no disagreement.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed font-serif m-0">
            Where Shentong diverges is at the level of <strong>ultimate reality</strong>. The Jonang tradition holds that when all adventitious defilements (ignorance, craving, dualistic thinking) are stripped away, what remains is not a blank void but a <strong>luminous, unconditioned primordial awareness (<em>jñāna</em>)</strong> that is <em>already endowed</em> with the spontaneous qualities of a fully awakened Buddha — omniscient wisdom, unobstructed compassion, and immutable bliss. This awareness is "empty of other" (<em>gzhan-stong</em>): it is free from all the contaminating defilements that are <em>other</em> to its nature, but it is <strong>not empty of its own enlightened qualities</strong>.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed font-serif m-0">
            Buddha-nature, on this view, is the golden statue already complete beneath the mud — every feature finished, nothing to add. The path is entirely subtractive: chipping away and washing off the obscurations until what was always there becomes visible. The qualities of a Buddha are <em>revealed</em>, not produced.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed font-serif m-0">
            The sūtras themselves supply these images. The <em>Tathāgatagarbha Sūtra</em>, elaborated in the <em>Ratnagotravibhāga</em> (<em>Uttaratantra</em>), offers nine classical similes for Buddha-nature obscured by defilements — a Buddha-image wrapped in rags, a gold coin fallen into filth, a treasure beneath a pauper's house, a seed containing a great tree. <strong>Both Rangtong and Shentong exegetes read the same similes</strong>. They disagree about whether to take them literally as descriptions of an already-present reality (<em>nītārtha</em>, definitive meaning — the Shentong reading) or as skillful provisional pointers toward mere emptiness-as-workability (<em>neyārtha</em>, requiring interpretive deconstruction — the Rangtong reading). This interpretive move — not the imagery — is where the argument actually lives. The Third Turning sūtras are taken by the Jonang as the Buddha's <strong>definitive, final teaching</strong>; by the Gelug, as provisional encouragement needing further analysis.
          </p>
          <div class="pt-3 border-t border-border text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed space-y-1">
            <span class="text-accent-secondary font-bold block text-caption-xs uppercase mb-1">What Shentong affirms that Rangtong does not:</span>
            <p class="m-0">That ultimate reality is not a mere absence but a <em>presence</em> — an unconditioned awareness possessing inseparable enlightened qualities (wisdom, compassion, bliss) that have always been there, merely obscured by temporary mental stains. Removing the stains does not create Buddhahood; it reveals what was never lost.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>

      <!-- Concise Summary Callout -->
      <div class="surface-card p-6 border-l-4 border-l-border space-y-3">
        <h4 class="text-base font-display font-bold text-ink m-0">The Core Distinction in One Sentence:</h4>
        <p class="text-sm text-ink leading-relaxed m-0 font-serif">
          <strong>Rangtong</strong> says: <em>"Everything, including the ultimate, is empty of its own nature — there is nothing positive behind the negation."</em> <br />
          <strong>Shentong</strong> says: <em>"Conventional things are empty of their own nature, but ultimate reality is a luminous awareness empty only of the temporary stains that cover it — and that awareness is replete with the qualities of Buddhahood."</em>
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0 font-serif">
          Both views reject the existence of an ordinary, personal ego-self (<em>ātman</em> in the Hindu sense). The Shentong "True Self" (<em>paramātman</em>) referenced in certain sūtras is not a soul, but the total transcendence of all conceptual limitations — the unconditioned nature of mind itself.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Rangtong vs Shentong Detailed Comparison Table -->
      <div class="surface-card p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-6">
        <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-display text-ink font-normal m-0">
          Philosophical Matrix: Rangtong vs. Shentong
        </h3>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-border bg-surface-subtle">
                <th class="p-3.5 font-display font-semibold text-ink">Dimension</th>
                <th class="p-3.5 font-display font-semibold text-accent">Rangtong (Self-Emptiness)</th>
                <th class="p-3.5 font-display font-semibold text-accent-secondary">Shentong (Other-Emptiness)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-border">
              <tr class="hover:bg-surface-subtle/50 transition-colors">
                <td class="p-3.5 font-semibold text-ink align-top">Primary Institutional Home</td>
                <td class="p-3.5 text-muted align-top leading-relaxed">Gelug (and scholastic branches of Sakya)</td>
                <td class="p-3.5 text-ink align-top leading-relaxed font-medium">Jonang (with Kagyu & Nyingma contemplative variations)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="hover:bg-surface-subtle/50 transition-colors">
                <td class="p-3.5 font-semibold text-ink align-top">Definition of Emptiness</td>
                <td class="p-3.5 text-muted align-top leading-relaxed">Absolute negation of inherent existence across all phenomena (non-affirming negative).</td>
                <td class="p-3.5 text-ink align-top leading-relaxed font-medium">Negation of adventitious defilements from the ultimate, unconditioned reality.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="hover:bg-surface-subtle/50 transition-colors">
                <td class="p-3.5 font-semibold text-ink align-top">Status of Ultimate Reality</td>
                <td class="p-3.5 text-muted align-top leading-relaxed">The mere absence of intrinsic nature; unconstructed non-affirming negative.</td>
                <td class="p-3.5 text-ink align-top leading-relaxed font-medium">Non-dual, unconditioned primordial awareness (jñāna) endowed with inseparable enlightened qualities.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="hover:bg-surface-subtle/50 transition-colors">
                <td class="p-3.5 font-semibold text-ink align-top">Scriptural Authority</td>
                <td class="p-3.5 text-muted align-top leading-relaxed">Second Turning (Prajñāpāramitā Sūtras, Nāgārjuna’s Mūlamadhyamakakārikā).</td>
                <td class="p-3.5 text-ink align-top leading-relaxed font-medium">Third Turning (Tathāgatagarbha Sūtras, Maitreya’s Ratnagotravibhāga / Uttaratantra).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="hover:bg-surface-subtle/50 transition-colors">
                <td class="p-3.5 font-semibold text-ink align-top">Hermeneutical Stance</td>
                <td class="p-3.5 text-muted align-top leading-relaxed">Second Turning is definitive (nītārtha); Third Turning requires interpretive deconstruction.</td>
                <td class="p-3.5 text-ink align-top leading-relaxed font-medium">Third Turning is definitive (nītārtha); Second Turning is provisional (neyārtha) teaching on conventional reality.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- The 10 Definitive Sutras Grid -->
      <div class="surface-card p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-2">
          <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-display text-ink font-normal m-0">
            The Ten Definitive Third Turning Sūtras (Nītārtha)
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed m-0">
            Dölpopa Sherab Gyaltsen codified ten foundational Mahāyāna scriptures revealing the unconditioned reality of Buddha-nature:
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 pt-2">
          {tenDefinitiveSutras.map((s, idx) => (
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface-subtle border border-border space-y-1 hover-lift">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-mono text-accent font-bold">
                <span>#{idx + 1 < 10 ? `0${idx + 1}` : idx + 1}</span>
                <span class="text-ink font-display text-sm">{s.name}</span>
              </div>
              <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0">{s.desc}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

