    <!-- VII. Chapter 6: Crisis, Suppression & Modern Resurgence -->
    <section id="suppression-resurgence" class="space-y-8 scroll-mt-24">
      <ChapterHeader
        chapter="Chapter 06"
        title="Crisis, Suppression, and Modern Resurgence"
        lede="The Jonang school was a dominant intellectual and spiritual power in Central Tibet until 17th-century geopolitical conflicts transformed its trajectory:"
      />

      <!-- Timeline Narrative Grid -->
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
        
        <div class="surface-card p-6 space-y-3 border-l-4 border-l-accent hover-lift">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-accent uppercase">1642 – 1658</span>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-display font-bold text-ink leading-snug m-0">17th Century Suppression</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0 font-serif">
            Following civil war in Central Tibet between the Tsangpa dynasty and the Gelug-Mongol alliance under the 5th Dalai Lama and Güshi Khan, Jonang’s main monastery Takten Damchöling was forcibly converted. Shentong printing blocks were sealed or burned, leading historians outside Tibet to assume the tradition had vanished.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="surface-card p-6 space-y-3 border-l-4 border-l-gold hover-lift">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-accent uppercase">18th – 19th Century</span>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-display font-bold text-ink leading-snug m-0">Preservation in Amdo & Rimé</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0 font-serif">
            The tradition survived with unbroken vigor in the remote mountainous valleys of Amdo and Ngawa, centered around <strong>Dzamthang Monastery</strong>. In the 19th century, masters of the non-sectarian <strong>Rimé movement</strong> (Jamgön Kongtrül, Jamyang Khyentse Wangpo) actively revived Shentong study.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="surface-card p-6 space-y-3 border-l-4 border-l-accent-secondary hover-lift">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-accent uppercase">2011 – Present</span>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-display font-bold text-ink leading-snug m-0">Official Recognition & Today</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0 font-serif">
            In September 2011, H.H. the 14th Dalai Lama formally recognized Jonang as the fifth major living school of Tibetan Buddhism. Today, over 60 active monasteries support more than 10,000 monks and nuns, with Main Jonang Takten Phuntsok Choeling in Shimla serving as the lineage seat in exile.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

